implementationPlan.note:
SIX OPTIONAL FIELDS + THE CANONICAL RULE, in moe-vscode/src/types/moe.ts only.

Task interface (after planCritiqueResult) gained exactly the six members, nullability copied from packages/moe-daemon/src/types/schema.ts:747-791:
  needsHumanReview?: boolean;
  blockedReason?: string | null;
  blockedOnTaskIds?: string[] | null;
  blockedResourceId?: string | null;
  blockedFromStatus?: TaskStatus | null;
  blockedAt?: string | null;
All optional, so every existing construction site and every test fixture still compiles. No transport parser was added or changed — the raw daemon client keeps passing payload fields through untouched.

New "Blocker presentation" section (before `interface Worker`): `BlockCause` union, `BLOCKED_STATUS`, the label/clearance tables and the caption constants, all with the JetBrains wording verbatim (Resource wait / Dependency wait / External block; the three clears sentences; 'Awaiting human review'; 'Prerequisites recorded as waited on'; 'No reason was recorded.'). Then the pure functions: `blockedPrerequisiteIds`, `blockCause`, `needsHumanAttention`, plus `blockedReasonText` / `blockedResourceText` / `blockedFromStatusText` / `blockedAtText`.

The three rules, implemented exactly as copied in step 1: status gate first (`task.status !== 'BLOCKED'` returns null); inside BLOCKED a non-blank resource id wins, then a non-empty list of non-blank ids, else EXTERNAL_BLOCK; `needsHumanAttention` reads `task.needsHumanReview === true` verbatim and is never derived from status, reason or critique.

Error handling in this copy: one private `nonBlankString(value: unknown)` guards `typeof value === 'string' && value.trim() !== ''`, so a missing, null or wrong-typed field yields no cause instead of throwing or inventing a wait. A blank/whitespace resource id falls through to the dependency branch. `blockedPrerequisiteIds` returns [] unless `Array.isArray`, filters blanks BEFORE the non-empty test, and keeps order + duplicates + verbatim ids. `blockedAtText` only trims-tests; blockedAt is never parsed as a date and no clock is read anywhere.

Trade-off recorded in the file's own doc comment: the two webview scripts are plain browser files with no bundler, so they cannot import this module and must carry their own copy of the rule. A sixth shared module would break the five-file scope. The mitigation is the step-6 drift guard driving one fixture table through all three copies.

Verification: `npm --prefix moe-vscode run compile` exit 0 (tsc -p ./). `npm --prefix moe-vscode test` still tests 26 / pass 24 / fail 2 — the same two intended reds, no new failure and no existing test disturbed.
